WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.110
In 2009, District 9 became the surprise hit
movie of the year.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:10.960
On a budget of just $30 million, the movie
went on to make $210 million at the worldwide

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.560
box office and was nominated for four Academy
Awards, including Best Motion Picture.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.940
Although it didn't win any Oscars, District
9's breakout success was an impressive feat

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:25.820
for an indie sci-fi film out of South Africa
with a first-time writer/director that featured

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:27.199
no movie stars.

00:00:27.199 --> 00:00:30.280
So why wasn't a sequel to District 9 ever
made?

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.500
Here's why.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.539
A standalone story

00:00:33.539 --> 00:00:38.659
According to District 9 mastermind Neill Blomkamp,
he wasn't even thinking about making a sequel

00:00:38.659 --> 00:00:41.190
while writing, directing, and editing the
film.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:45.469
Since Blomkamp was only planning on making
a standalone film, he didn't set up the overall

00:00:45.469 --> 00:00:48.120
story arc was for a sequel or a franchise.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.710
It was only after the film was edited that
Blomkamp realized there was room for a sequel,

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:55.329
but he still had no idea where that sequel
would go.

00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:58.549
He also toyed around with making a prequel
instead.

00:00:58.549 --> 00:01:00.309
Blomkamp told Hero Complex in 2010:

00:01:00.309 --> 00:01:04.879
"I'm not so interested in aliens coming back
and blowing things up but [a prequel] might

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:05.990
be interesting."

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:11.110
Obviously that idea never left the ground
either, and it wasn't until 2015 that Blomkamp

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:14.759
said that he had a "really cool idea" for
a District 9 sequel.

00:01:14.759 --> 00:01:16.950
Does that mean that a sequel is finally on
the way?

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.159
At the moment, it doesn't look likely.

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:21.340
Original films

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:25.719
In a perfect world, production on a District
9 sequel would have started shortly after

00:01:25.719 --> 00:01:27.200
the first one was released.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.670
However, without an idea for the sequel, Blomkamp
instead used the success of District 9 to

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:37.479
get another original sci-fi film into production
within months of District 9's release.

00:01:37.479 --> 00:01:40.909
That movie was Elysium, which he worked on
until late 2012.

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:47.600
While shooting Elysium, Blomkamp and his wife/co-screenwriter,
Terri Tatchell, wrote the script for Chappie

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.749
in two weeks, and after Elysium wrapped, they
immediately started working on that.

00:01:52.749 --> 00:01:57.710
Production on that movie lasted into 2014,
and it was released in theaters in February

00:01:57.710 --> 00:01:58.799
2015.

00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:03.690
So yeah, those movies kept Blomkamp pretty
busy post-District 9.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:07.530
Blomkamp told Rolling Stone that if he were
to do the sequel properly, it would take three

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:08.690
years to make.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:13.070
That's a big time commitment, all while the
movie that set it all off recedes into fans'

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.770
memories...

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:16.150
Other projects

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:20.830
Chappie was released in May 2015, and although
there hasn't been much news about the director

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:23.770
since then, Blomkamp has definitely been busy.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:29.220
In April 2017, Blomkamp revealed on Twitter
that he would be making "experimental" short

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.520
films for the video game platform, Steam.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.690
That's a big change from big-budget sci-fi
flicks, although at this point in his career

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:40.190
it's certainly less risky than taking a chance
on a sequel to District 9.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:44.640
Blomkamp was also briefly attached to direct
a fifth entry in the Alien series, going so

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.550
far as to release tantalizing unofficial concept
art on Instagram, but unfortunately, that

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.910
project probably isn't happening.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:56.710
In January 2017, Blomkamp tweeted the awful
truth: the chances of his Alien movie ever

00:02:56.710 --> 00:03:01.430
moving forward are "slim," echoing an earlier
tweet that the movie was set aside pending

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:04.060
the success or failure of Alien: Covenant.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:07.070
That doesn't mean that Blomkamp's blockbuster
days are over.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:11.050
After all, this wouldn't be the first time
Blomkamp has bounced back after being sidelined

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:13.430
on a high-profile film project.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:17.620
Originally, Blomkamp was picked by Peter Jackson
to direct a movie based on the Halo video

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:18.860
game franchise.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:24.490
When Halo fell through, Jackson agreed to
produce District 9 based on Blomkamp's 2006

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:26.250
short film "Alive in Joberg."

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:30.510
In other words, the fact that Blomkamp hasn't
made a movie for several years now doesn't

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:36.420
mean that a District 9 sequel will never happen...although
it seems to be getting less and less likely.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.340
Sharlto Copley is busy, too

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:43.190
Although he looks different at the end of
the film, Sharlto Copley is the face of District

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:44.190
9.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:48.700
In his breakout role as the delightfully clueless
bureaucrat, the South African actor wowed

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.540
international audiences and launched his Hollywood
career.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.050
"But I don't trust it.

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:55.090
I don't trust anything tha-...

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:56.090
Pwwwwww!"

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:57.190
"You alright mate?"

00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:58.760
"Vickers, you alright?"

00:03:58.760 --> 00:03:59.820
"Cut it!"

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:04.280
After Chappie, Copley's third collaboration
with Neill Blomkamp, the actor went on to

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:09.260
star in the Playstation series Powers and
the action thriller Free Fire.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:13.390
As busy as he is, though, Copley has made
it clear that he'd gladly reprise his role

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:16.050
if a District 9 sequel ever materialized.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.360
Too hard to follow up?

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.950
District 9 ended on a cliffhanger, and the
universe of the film is rich with themes and

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:24.850
storylines to explore.

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:29.570
Copley wants to do a sequel, and the movie
was a huge success for Peter Jackson's production

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:30.570
company.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:34.060
In Hollywood, that combination usually adds
up to a guaranteed sequel.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:39.360
And yet the furthest in development that the
District 9 sequel got was an 18-page treatment.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.200
One reason for that may be because District
9 was too big of a hit.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.880
Its commentary on social segregation, xenophobia,
and racism blended perfectly with its sci-fi

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.130
aesthetic, all of which helped make it an
overwhelming success for the first-time writer/director.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:58.080
That's a hard act to follow — even when
the act you're following is your own.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:04:59.190
Thanks for watching!

